  "live_records": {
    "q-74970": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74970",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection.",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
